WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.583 --> 00:00:07.462
It's getting harder, isn't it,
to spot real from fake,

00:00:07.504 --> 00:00:09.756
AI-generated from human-generated.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:11.466
With generative AI,

00:00:11.508 --> 00:00:13.927
along with other advances in deep fakery,

00:00:13.969 --> 00:00:16.221
it doesn't take
many seconds of your voice,

00:00:16.263 --> 00:00:17.722
many images of your face,

00:00:17.764 --> 00:00:19.015
to fake you,

00:00:19.015 --> 00:00:21.643
and the realism keeps increasing.

00:00:21.685 --> 00:00:24.771
I first started working
on deepfakes in 2017,

00:00:24.813 --> 00:00:28.775
when the threat to our trust
in information was overhyped,

00:00:28.775 --> 00:00:32.445
and the big harm, in reality,
was falsified sexual images.

00:00:32.904 --> 00:00:37.075
Now that problem keeps growing,
harming women and girls worldwide.

00:00:38.159 --> 00:00:42.872
But also, with advances in generative AI,
we're now also approaching a world

00:00:42.872 --> 00:00:46.334
where it's broadly easier
to make fake reality,

00:00:46.376 --> 00:00:50.255
but also to dismiss reality
as possibly faked.

00:00:50.755 --> 00:00:54.175
Now, deceptive and malicious
audiovisual AI

00:00:54.217 --> 00:00:56.886
is not the root of our societal problems,

00:00:56.928 --> 00:00:59.180
but it's likely to contribute to them.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.393
Audio clones are proliferating
in a range of electoral contexts.

00:01:03.435 --> 00:01:08.565
"Is it, isn't it" claims cloud
human-rights evidence from war zones,

00:01:08.565 --> 00:01:12.694
sexual deepfakes target women
in public and in private,

00:01:12.736 --> 00:01:16.072
and synthetic avatars
impersonate news anchors.

00:01:16.656 --> 00:01:18.116
I lead WITNESS.

00:01:18.116 --> 00:01:19.492
We're a human-rights group

00:01:19.492 --> 00:01:23.163
that helps people use video and technology
to protect and defend their rights.

00:01:23.163 --> 00:01:26.166
And for the last five years,
we've coordinated a global effort,

00:01:26.333 --> 00:01:27.500
"Prepare, Don't Panic,"

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.545
around these new ways
to manipulate and synthesize reality,

00:01:30.587 --> 00:01:32.964
and on how to fortify the truth

00:01:32.964 --> 00:01:36.384
of critical frontline journalists
and human-rights defenders.

00:01:37.218 --> 00:01:42.641
Now, one element in that
is a deepfakes rapid-response task force,

00:01:42.641 --> 00:01:44.768
made up of media-forensics experts

00:01:44.768 --> 00:01:46.936
and companies who donate
their time and skills

00:01:46.978 --> 00:01:50.065
to debunk deepfakes
and claims of deepfakes.

00:01:50.899 --> 00:01:54.110
The task force recently received
three audio clips,

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:56.780
from Sudan, West Africa and India.

00:01:57.155 --> 00:02:01.034
People were claiming that the clips
were deepfaked, not real.

00:02:01.451 --> 00:02:02.661
In the Sudan case,

00:02:02.702 --> 00:02:04.704
experts used a machine-learning algorithm

00:02:04.746 --> 00:02:07.374
trained on over a million examples
of synthetic speech

00:02:07.374 --> 00:02:09.668
to prove, almost
without a shadow of a doubt,

00:02:09.709 --> 00:02:11.044
that it was authentic.

00:02:11.586 --> 00:02:13.421
In the West Africa case,

00:02:13.463 --> 00:02:15.465
they couldn't reach
a definitive conclusion

00:02:15.507 --> 00:02:18.301
because of the challenges
of analyzing audio from Twitter,

00:02:18.301 --> 00:02:20.053
and with background noise.

00:02:20.095 --> 00:02:23.807
The third clip was leaked audio
of a politician from India.

00:02:23.848 --> 00:02:27.644
Nilesh Christopher of “Rest of World”
brought the case to the task force.

00:02:27.644 --> 00:02:30.605
The experts used almost an hour of samples

00:02:30.605 --> 00:02:34.484
to develop a personalized model
of the politician's authentic voice.

00:02:35.151 --> 00:02:39.531
Despite his loud and fast claims
that it was all falsified with AI,

00:02:39.572 --> 00:02:43.827
experts concluded that it at least
was partially real, not AI.

00:02:44.369 --> 00:02:45.704
As you can see,

00:02:45.745 --> 00:02:50.834
even experts cannot rapidly
and conclusively separate true from false,

00:02:50.875 --> 00:02:55.296
and the ease of calling
"that's deepfaked" on something real

00:02:55.296 --> 00:02:56.464
is increasing.

00:02:57.132 --> 00:02:59.134
The future is full of profound challenges,

00:02:59.175 --> 00:03:02.595
both in protecting the real
and detecting the fake.

00:03:03.888 --> 00:03:05.807
We're already seeing the warning signs

00:03:05.807 --> 00:03:08.518
of this challenge
of discerning fact from fiction.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.688
Audio and video deepfakes
have targeted politicians,

00:03:11.688 --> 00:03:15.275
major political leaders in the EU,
Turkey and Mexico,

00:03:15.316 --> 00:03:17.026
and US mayoral candidates.

00:03:17.444 --> 00:03:20.947
Political ads are incorporating footage
of events that never happened,

00:03:20.947 --> 00:03:25.493
and people are sharing
AI-generated imagery from crisis zones,

00:03:25.535 --> 00:03:26.953
claiming it to be real.

00:03:27.454 --> 00:03:30.665
Now, again, this problem
is not entirely new.

00:03:31.207 --> 00:03:33.835
The human-rights defenders
and journalists I work with

00:03:33.835 --> 00:03:36.629
are used to having
their stories dismissed,

00:03:36.671 --> 00:03:40.133
and they're used to widespread,
deceptive, shallow fakes,

00:03:40.175 --> 00:03:43.845
videos and images taken
from one context or time or place

00:03:43.887 --> 00:03:46.347
and claimed as if they're in another,

00:03:46.347 --> 00:03:49.476
used to share confusion
and spread disinformation.

00:03:49.934 --> 00:03:53.104
And of course, we live in a world
that is full of partisanship

00:03:53.146 --> 00:03:55.273
and plentiful confirmation bias.

00:03:57.317 --> 00:03:58.526
Given all that,

00:03:58.526 --> 00:04:01.571
the last thing we need
is a diminishing baseline

00:04:01.613 --> 00:04:05.575
of the shared, trustworthy information
upon which democracies thrive,

00:04:05.617 --> 00:04:07.035
where the specter of AI

00:04:07.076 --> 00:04:10.538
is used to plausibly believe
things you want to believe,

00:04:10.538 --> 00:04:12.874
and plausibly deny things
you want to ignore.

00:04:15.084 --> 00:04:17.712
But I think there's a way
we can prevent that future,

00:04:17.754 --> 00:04:19.255
if we act now;

00:04:19.255 --> 00:04:21.466
that if we "Prepare, Don't Panic,"

00:04:21.508 --> 00:04:24.761
we'll kind of make our way
through this somehow.

00:04:25.929 --> 00:04:28.556
Panic won't serve us well.

00:04:28.681 --> 00:04:31.392
[It] plays into the hands
of governments and corporations

00:04:31.392 --> 00:04:33.061
who will abuse our fears,

00:04:33.102 --> 00:04:36.147
and into the hands of people
who want a fog of confusion

00:04:36.147 --> 00:04:38.608
and will use AI as an excuse.

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:43.029
How many people were taken in,
just for a minute,

00:04:43.029 --> 00:04:45.365
by the Pope in his dripped-out
puffer jacket?

00:04:45.406 --> 00:04:46.574
You can admit it.

00:04:46.574 --> 00:04:47.784
(Laughter)

00:04:47.784 --> 00:04:48.993
More seriously,

00:04:49.035 --> 00:04:51.538
how many of you know someone
who's been scammed

00:04:51.579 --> 00:04:53.623
by an audio that sounds like their kid?

00:04:54.624 --> 00:04:57.544
And for those of you
who are thinking "I wasn't taken in,

00:04:57.544 --> 00:04:59.128
I know how to spot a deepfake,"

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:02.173
any tip you know now is already outdated.

00:05:02.757 --> 00:05:05.301
Deepfakes didn't blink, they do now.

00:05:06.177 --> 00:05:09.764
Six-fingered hands were more common
in deepfake land than real life --

00:05:09.806 --> 00:05:10.932
not so much.

00:05:11.307 --> 00:05:15.061
Technical advances erase
those visible and audible clues

00:05:15.061 --> 00:05:17.063
that we so desperately want to hang on to

00:05:17.105 --> 00:05:19.357
as proof we can discern real from fake.

00:05:20.191 --> 00:05:24.904
But it also really shouldn’t be on us
to make that guess without any help.

00:05:24.946 --> 00:05:27.073
Between real deepfakes
and claimed deepfakes,

00:05:27.073 --> 00:05:30.034
we need big-picture, structural solutions.

00:05:30.034 --> 00:05:31.536
We need robust foundations

00:05:31.578 --> 00:05:34.706
that enable us to discern
authentic from simulated,

00:05:34.747 --> 00:05:38.668
tools to fortify the credibility
of critical voices and images,

00:05:38.668 --> 00:05:41.004
and powerful detection technology

00:05:41.045 --> 00:05:43.715
that doesn't raise more doubts
than it fixes.

00:05:45.091 --> 00:05:48.136
There are three steps we need to take
to get to that future.

00:05:48.887 --> 00:05:52.599
Step one is to ensure
that the detection skills and tools

00:05:52.599 --> 00:05:54.767
are in the hands
of the people who need them.

00:05:54.767 --> 00:05:57.020
I've talked to hundreds of journalists,

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:59.272
community leaders
and human-rights defenders,

00:05:59.272 --> 00:06:02.191
and they're in the same boat
as you and me and us.

00:06:02.191 --> 00:06:05.612
They're listening to the audio,
trying to think, "Can I spot a glitch?"

00:06:05.612 --> 00:06:08.907
Looking at the image, saying,
"Oh, does that look right or not?"

00:06:08.907 --> 00:06:12.243
Or maybe they're going online
to find a detector.

00:06:12.285 --> 00:06:13.620
And the detector they find,

00:06:13.661 --> 00:06:17.206
they don't know whether they're getting
a false positive, a false negative,

00:06:17.248 --> 00:06:18.499
or a reliable result.

00:06:18.541 --> 00:06:19.709
Here's an example.

00:06:19.751 --> 00:06:23.463
I used a detector, which got the Pope
in the puffer jacket right.

00:06:23.796 --> 00:06:28.051
But then, when I put in the Easter bunny
image that I made for my kids,

00:06:28.092 --> 00:06:30.053
it said that it was human-generated.

00:06:30.637 --> 00:06:33.890
This is because of some big challenges
in deepfake detection.

00:06:34.474 --> 00:06:37.769
Detection tools often only work
on one single way to make a deepfake,

00:06:37.769 --> 00:06:39.312
so you need multiple tools,

00:06:39.354 --> 00:06:43.691
and they don't work well
on low-quality social media content.

00:06:43.691 --> 00:06:47.028
Confidence score, 0.76-0.87,

00:06:47.028 --> 00:06:48.947
how do you know whether that's reliable,

00:06:48.988 --> 00:06:51.783
if you don't know if the underlying
technology is reliable,

00:06:51.824 --> 00:06:54.619
or whether it works on the manipulation
that is being used?

00:06:54.661 --> 00:06:59.707
And tools to spot an AI manipulation
don't spot a manual edit.

00:07:00.583 --> 00:07:04.170
These tools also won't be
available to everyone.

00:07:04.212 --> 00:07:07.340
There's a trade-off
between security and access,

00:07:07.382 --> 00:07:09.926
which means if we make them
available to anyone,

00:07:09.926 --> 00:07:12.512
they become useless to everybody,

00:07:12.512 --> 00:07:15.223
because the people designing
the new deception techniques

00:07:15.264 --> 00:07:18.351
will test them on the publicly
available detectors

00:07:18.393 --> 00:07:19.602
and evade them.

00:07:20.061 --> 00:07:22.981
But we do need to make sure
these are available

00:07:22.981 --> 00:07:25.066
to the journalists, the community leaders,

00:07:25.108 --> 00:07:28.736
the election officials, globally,
who are our first line of defense,

00:07:28.736 --> 00:07:31.990
thought through with attention
to real-world accessibility and use.

00:07:32.991 --> 00:07:35.535
Though at the best circumstances,

00:07:35.576 --> 00:07:38.579
detection tools will be
85 to 95 percent effective,

00:07:38.579 --> 00:07:41.374
they have to be in the hands
of that first line of defense,

00:07:41.374 --> 00:07:42.917
and they're not, right now.

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:46.838
So for step one, I've been talking
about detection after the fact.

00:07:46.838 --> 00:07:51.300
Step two -- AI is going to be everywhere
in our communication,

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:53.469
creating, changing, editing.

00:07:53.469 --> 00:07:58.224
It's not going to be a simple binary
of "yes, it's AI" or "phew, it's not."

00:07:58.224 --> 00:08:01.310
AI is part of all of our communication,

00:08:01.352 --> 00:08:05.398
so we need to better understand
the recipe of what we're consuming.

00:08:06.232 --> 00:08:09.986
Some people call this
content provenance and disclosure.

00:08:10.028 --> 00:08:13.531
Technologists have been building ways
to add invisible watermarking

00:08:13.573 --> 00:08:15.450
to AI-generated media.

00:08:15.491 --> 00:08:17.243
They've also been designing ways --

00:08:17.243 --> 00:08:19.120
and I've been part of these efforts --

00:08:19.162 --> 00:08:20.872
within a standard called the C2PA,

00:08:20.872 --> 00:08:23.541
to add cryptographically signed
metadata to files.

00:08:24.125 --> 00:08:28.504
This means data that provides details
about the content,

00:08:28.546 --> 00:08:32.258
cryptographically signed
in a way that reinforces our trust

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:33.801
in that information.

00:08:33.801 --> 00:08:39.098
It's an updating record
of how AI was used to create or edit it,

00:08:39.098 --> 00:08:41.768
where humans and other
technologies were involved,

00:08:41.809 --> 00:08:43.728
and how it was distributed.

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:46.773
It's basically a recipe
and serving instructions

00:08:46.814 --> 00:08:48.775
for the mix of AI and human

00:08:48.816 --> 00:08:51.152
that's in what you're seeing and hearing.

00:08:51.903 --> 00:08:56.365
And it's a critical part of a new
AI-infused media literacy.

00:08:57.116 --> 00:08:59.577
And this actually
shouldn't sound that crazy.

00:08:59.577 --> 00:09:02.789
Our communication is moving
in this direction already.

00:09:02.789 --> 00:09:04.791
If you're like me -- you can admit it --

00:09:04.832 --> 00:09:07.251
you browse your TikTok “For You” page,

00:09:07.251 --> 00:09:11.464
and you're used to seeing videos
that have an audio source,

00:09:11.464 --> 00:09:13.883
an AI filter, a green screen,
a background,

00:09:13.883 --> 00:09:15.635
a stitch with another edit.

00:09:15.676 --> 00:09:19.013
This, in some sense, is the alpha version
of this transparency

00:09:19.055 --> 00:09:21.432
in some of the major
platforms we use today.

00:09:21.474 --> 00:09:24.227
It's just that it does not yet travel
across the internet,

00:09:24.268 --> 00:09:27.396
it’s not reliable, updatable, 
and it’s not secure.

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:30.608
Now, there are also big challenges

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:33.194
in this type of infrastructure
for authenticity.

00:09:34.278 --> 00:09:38.366
As we create these durable signs
of how AI and human were mixed,

00:09:38.366 --> 00:09:41.452
that carry across the trajectory
of how media is made,

00:09:41.494 --> 00:09:45.623
we need to ensure they don't compromise
privacy or backfire globally.

00:09:46.249 --> 00:09:47.959
We have to get this right.

00:09:48.584 --> 00:09:52.839
We can't oblige a citizen journalist
filming in a repressive context

00:09:52.839 --> 00:09:56.008
or a satirical maker 
using novel gen-AI tools

00:09:56.008 --> 00:09:57.260
to parody the powerful ...

00:09:58.845 --> 00:10:03.724
to have to disclose their identity
or personally identifiable information

00:10:03.766 --> 00:10:06.727
in order to use their camera or ChatGPT.

00:10:08.312 --> 00:10:12.024
Because it's important they be able
to retain their ability to have anonymity,

00:10:12.066 --> 00:10:15.444
at the same time as the tool
to create is transparent.

00:10:16.112 --> 00:10:20.283
This needs to be about the how
of AI-human media making,

00:10:20.283 --> 00:10:21.450
not the who.

00:10:22.952 --> 00:10:25.163
This brings me to the final step.

00:10:25.163 --> 00:10:29.625
None of this works
without a pipeline of responsibility

00:10:29.667 --> 00:10:33.921
that runs from the foundation models
and the open-source projects

00:10:33.963 --> 00:10:38.176
through to the way that is deployed
into systems, APIs and apps,

00:10:38.217 --> 00:10:41.596
to the platforms where we consume
media and communicate.

00:10:43.139 --> 00:10:47.310
I've spent much of the last 15 years
fighting, essentially, a rearguard action,

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:50.229
like so many of my colleagues
in the human rights world,

00:10:50.229 --> 00:10:52.398
against the failures of social media.

00:10:52.899 --> 00:10:58.279
We can't make those mistakes again
in this next generation of technology.

00:10:59.572 --> 00:11:01.407
What this means is that governments

00:11:01.449 --> 00:11:05.703
need to ensure that within this pipeline
of responsibility for AI,

00:11:05.703 --> 00:11:08.956
there is transparency,
accountability and liability.

00:11:10.666 --> 00:11:12.752
Without these three steps --

00:11:12.793 --> 00:11:15.922
detection for the people who need it most,

00:11:15.922 --> 00:11:18.424
provenance that is rights-respecting

00:11:18.466 --> 00:11:20.635
and that pipeline of responsibility,

00:11:20.635 --> 00:11:24.180
we're going to get stuck looking in vain
for the six-fingered hand,

00:11:24.222 --> 00:11:25.765
or the eyes that don't blink.

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:28.226
We need to take these steps.

00:11:28.226 --> 00:11:31.520
Otherwise, we risk a world
where it gets easier and easier

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:33.022
to both fake reality

00:11:33.064 --> 00:11:35.733
and dismiss reality as potentially faked.

00:11:36.234 --> 00:11:39.320
And that is a world that the political
philosopher Hannah Arendt

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:40.780
described in these terms:

00:11:40.821 --> 00:11:43.449
"A people that no longer
can believe anything

00:11:43.491 --> 00:11:45.785
cannot make up its own mind.

00:11:45.785 --> 00:11:48.996
It is deprived not only
of its capacity to act

00:11:48.996 --> 00:11:52.500
but also of its capacity
to think and to judge.

00:11:52.959 --> 00:11:56.170
And with such a people
you can then do what you please."

00:11:56.712 --> 00:11:58.756
That's a world I know none of us want,

00:11:58.798 --> 00:12:00.800
that I think we can prevent.

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:01.968
Thanks.

00:12:02.009 --> 00:12:04.553
(Cheers and applause)

